## Coding standards (Unity code root is `Assets/`; tests in `Assets/Tests/EditMode|PlayMode`)
- Doc comments on public APIs. Gameplay values are data-driven (external config), never hardcoded. Prefer dependency injection over singletons.
- Commits: Conventional Commits (`feat:` `fix:` `chore:` `docs:` `test:` `refactor:`) and reference the design doc or task id (`Story: EPIC-001-S02`). Enforced by `hooks/commit-msg`.
- Tests first for gameplay systems. Logic -> unit test; UI/visual -> retained screenshot; deterministic, isolated tests; never skip a failing test to get green.
- Every fix gets a regression test you have watched FAIL against the unfixed code before you trust it.
- Design docs: Markdown, one per mechanic in `design/gdd/`; section count depends on `modes.workflow` (see `.claude/docs/workflow-modes.md`).

## RUN-AND-OBSERVE RULE (not waived at minimal rigor)
**A parse check is not a run.** A story that changes anything the player can see or hear is NOT done until the game has been
**launched windowed and observed**, and a **screenshot is saved in `production/qa/evidence/<story-slug>/`** (retained, referenced from the evidence doc).
1. Launch windowed at 1280x720 using `commands.run` from `project.yaml`; never headless/batch/null-RHI (those skip rendering). Unity: built player or editor Play mode with `-screen-width 1280 -screen-height 720`, NOT `-batchmode`/`-nographics`.
2. Launch straight into the scene the story touched. 3. Let ~60 frames render, capture one, quit (Unity: `ScreenCapture.CaptureScreenshot(<abs path>)` from a bootstrap MonoBehaviour).
4. Open the PNG and compare with the acceptance criteria (clipped text, overflow, missing element, wrong colour). 5. Keep the file; a discarded screenshot is an assertion, not evidence.
6. Report one `Run result:` line. Full procedure: `.claude/docs/run-and-observe.md`. If no display is available say so; do not claim the check was done.

